  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+8?$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932041/assets/1adec10c20862d7015466efc5ded1e966e959ac3f6141163afa26802b502d49e.png)\n\n \n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932041/assets/9651fab70ad189764143d92f89938cff1293a390bfca693c567bb33220ff859e.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $8$ units up to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+8.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,7)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=8.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932041/assets/1d41ded0d251b46ad6f137b6686e31bfdad299c62a1496e0782ada5d400780bf.png)",
